    "result_text": "The change is complete and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization for owner creation, following the existing patterns in the codebase.\n\n**New class \u2014 `rest/validation/AddressNormalizer.java`**\nMirrors the existing `EmailNormalizer`/`TelephoneNormalizer` components (same package, same style). Single responsibility: turn a raw address into its canonical form.\n- `normalize(...)`: trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-word abbreviations (`ST`\u2192`STREET`, `RD`\u2192`ROAD`, `AVE`\u2192`AVENUE`).\n- `isBlank(...)`: reports whether the (normalized) value is empty, used by the required-field check.\n\n**Wiring \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner(...)`**\nInjected the normalizer via the constructor (alongside the other two normalizers) and, at the start of `addOwner`, normalized the mapped owner's address, rejected it with `400 BAD_REQUEST` if blank after normalization, then set the normalized value back on the owner **before** the household checks. Because the household duplicate detection (`existsOwnerByLastNameAndAddress`) and the shared household id (`shareHousehold`) both read `owner.getAddress()`, they now operate on \u2014 and persist \u2014 the normalized form, exactly as the spec requires.\n\nI placed the normalization logic in its own small unit rather than inflating the controller or `HouseholdIdGenerator`, and reused the same constructor-injection/component idiom already established for email and telephone.\n\n**Tests:** `./mvnw test` \u2192 `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS (covers whitespace/case normalization, abbreviation expansion, and blank-after-normalization rejection).\n\nOne thing worth noting: the OpenAPI-generated bean validation on `address` (`pattern: '.*\\S.*'`) already rejects whitespace-only input with a 400 before the controller runs, so the explicit post-normalization blank check is a belt-and-suspenders guard that makes the required-field rule hold at the service boundary regardless of the DTO constraints. I scoped normalization to owner *creation* only, per the spec (\"whenever an owner is created\"); `updateOwner` was intentionally left unchanged.",
